7
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:29,840
He knows a lot about the science stuff, Professor Dave explains.

8
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:35,780
Over the past several tutorials, we've performed a few separation techniques, those being filtration

9
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:37,080
and extraction.

10
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:39,720
Now let's look at another one, distillation.

11
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:44,320
This is a technique that is very common in chemistry labs as well as industry, as it

12
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:49,760
is an effective and inexpensive way to separate liquid components of a mixture by virtue of

13
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,560
their boiling points.

14
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:55,960
So this is different from extraction, where the two solvents were immiscible, and we could

15
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:59,200
easily separate them with a separatory funnel.

16
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,520
Here we have a mixture of miscible liquids that have sufficiently different boiling points,

17
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:07,680
such that we can boil one and not the other.

18
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:11,700
This is achieved by heating the mixture to a temperature above the boiling point of

19
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,080
one component, but below the boiling point of the other, so that the liquid with the

20
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:20,520
lower boiling point turns into vapor, leaving the other liquid behind.

21
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:25,740
We can then collect, cool, and condense the vapor into a pure liquid on the other side

22
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:28,640
of the condenser in a receiving flask.

23
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:33,040
For this to be achieved, a significant difference in boiling point is required, otherwise too

24
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:36,420
much of the other component will vaporize as well.

25
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:40,500
For a simple distillation setup, we'll need a heat source such as a hot plate or

26
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:41,500
Bunsen burner.

27
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:47,480
We'll also need a round bottom flask, a three-way adapter like this one, a thermometer

28
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:55,680
adapter, a thermometer, a condenser, a vacuum adapter, and something with which to collect

29
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:02,120
the condensing vapor, also called the distillate, such as a beaker or Erlenmeyer flask.

30
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:07,800
We'll also need a support adapter for our round bottom flask, and some connector clips.

31
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:12,650
Lastly, we'll need ring stands and some clamps.

32
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:15,510
To build the setup, let's start with our round bottom flask.

33
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:20,390
We'll place it on a round bottom support and put that on a hot plate.

34
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:25,590
On top of the flask, we can add the three-way adapter with a side arm like this.

35
00:02:25,590 --> 00:02:33,870
We'll then connect the thermometer on top using a thermometer adapter.

36
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:38,530
We need to make sure that everything is sealed so that our vapor does not escape.

37
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:42,990
You can add some grease to the joints and turn them so everything fits well.

38
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:47,830
Your thermometer should be placed at a level that is just a bit lower than your side arm

39
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:50,990
to get an accurate measurement of the temperature of the vapor.

40
00:02:50,990 --> 00:02:57,500
Let's then secure our flask by clamping it at the top like this.

41
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:01,980
Now on this arm of the three-way adapter, we can add the condenser.

42
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:06,920
Since the condenser tends to fall off as gravity will cause it to slide down, we also

43
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:08,780
need to secure it.

44
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:14,580
We can do that by clamping our vacuum adapter like this and connecting it to the condenser.

45
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:20,280
We now have support on both sides.

46
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:25,100
To keep the condenser cool, we will run cold water through this outer chamber.

47
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:30,000
The vapor will move through the intersection of the condenser, and as it's being cooled

48
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,560
by contact with the glass that has been cooled by the cold water in the outer section, it

49
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:38,440
will begin to return to the liquid phase.

50
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:42,980
To enable the flow of cold water, on the bottom part of the condenser, let's connect

51
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:46,680
a hose that brings in cold water from the tap.

52
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:51,880
Then connect another hose where the water will exit on the top, and just let that empty into

53
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:52,880
the sink.

54
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:58,120
Let's then finish the setup by adding our collector underneath the end of the condenser

55
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:01,200
to collect the distillate as it drips out.

56
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:05,720
In this particular example, we are going to be using a graduated cylinder here because

57
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:09,940
we want to measure the volume of our distillate as time goes by.

58
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:11,960
So this is our complete setup.

59
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:16,600
Make sure you examine every joint to ensure that you have a tight seal.

60
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:20,940
Also check your clamps and adapters to ensure that nothing is loose.

61
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:25,640
We can further secure our setup by adding clips that look like this.

62
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:31,440
These can be used to clamp all the joints together so that the glassware does not separate.

63
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:37,520
You can now turn on the cold water and make sure that it flows smoothly through the condenser.

64
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:40,400
We are now ready to start the distillation.

65
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:46,160
Today we will be distilling a 50-50 mixture of isopropyl alcohol and water, with some

66
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,120
dye added to it.

67
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:50,760
That will give our solution some nice color.

68
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:57,620
The boiling point of isopropyl alcohol is 82.5 degrees Celsius, while water is 100,

69
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:01,440
so the alcohol is the substance that will vaporize here.

70
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:06,200
Additionally, because the dye is much more soluble in water than in the alcohol, our

71
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,840
distillate will be clear, leaving all of the dye behind in the water, acting as a

72
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:14,520
visual confirmation that separation is occurring.

73
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:19,840
When adding your solution to the round bottom flask, the liquid level should be around halfway

74
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:25,400
up the flask or lower, as we do not want any liquid to splatter up into the condenser.

75
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:30,500
Next, let's add a stirring bar so that we get even heating in our mixture.

76
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:35,360
We can also add a boiling chip to make the mixture boil more calmly.

77
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:40,480
Let's turn on the stirring function and start out by stirring the solution at a

78
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:42,740
slow rate like this.

79
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:48,100
We don't want to go too slow or too fast, so let's put this one at around 5.

80
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:52,180
Be sure to experiment with your stirring function, as different instruments will give

81
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:54,760
you different stirring speeds.

82
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:58,980
We can then double check that everything is connected, and that our water is running

83
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:00,780
through the condenser.

84
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:02,500
Now let's turn on the heater.

85
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:06,620
We'll turn this up to a medium level to get even heating.

86
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:10,900
Do not use a high temperature because the solution will start bubbling violently when

87
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:12,320
it boils.

88
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:17,000
If that happens, lower the temperature or remove the heat source completely, and wait

89
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,080
until everything cools down before you start again.

90
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:25,040
Also, the discrepancy in the two boiling points is not enormous, so we want to make sure

91
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:30,280
we don't heat to a temperature so high that both substances end up boiling.

92
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:35,400
If you set up the heating and stirring correctly, the mixture should soon come to a nice even

93
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:36,400
boil.

94
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:40,880
Vapor will rise up into the three-way adapter, where it will start condensing.

95
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:45,980
Eventually, vapor will start collecting in the top arm of the condenser.

96
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:50,820
The vapor will then travel through the condenser where it will begin to condense, and this

97
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:54,440
liquid will start falling into the collection vessel.

98
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:59,800
The isopropyl alcohol being collected is a highly flammable substance that can cause serious

99
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:04,820
eye irritation and damage to your skin, so if doing distillation of this type, make

100
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:09,600
sure you wear protective equipment and stay away from fire or sparks.

101
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:15,880
For this particular experiment, to add a component of data analysis, we can also collect some measurements.

102
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:21,000
For every milliliter of distillate collected, write down the total volume collected and

103
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,540
the temperature of the vapor as shown by the thermometer.

104
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:29,160
We should keep collecting the distillate until we get the amount of alcohol we originally

105
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:31,760
measured and added to the mixture.

106
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:36,000
The temperature should go up as the volume of distillate goes up.

107
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,300
This is because the composition of the mixture changes as more of one component is vaporized

108
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,880
and removed.

109
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:48,000
When all the alcohol has been collected, the condensation should stop, and the temperature

110
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,800
should stop rising.

111
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:53,980
A graph of your data for a simple distillation should look like this.

112
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:58,540
The temperature goes up as more distillate is collected, and it will level off after

113
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:01,080
we've removed all the alcohol.

114
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:06,400
If you know the identity of your target compound as we do in this case, you should collect your

115
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:11,240
distillate until you get to a range that is slightly above its boiling point.

116
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:15,660
You can then change the flask when the temperature goes much higher than that.

117
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:20,180
This is to avoid impurities that have a boiling point higher than your desired product, such

118
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:22,800
as water in this case.

119
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:28,080
And with that, we have completed a distillation in which we isolated isopropyl alcohol from

120
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:29,760
the rest of the mixture.

121
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:34,760
And as expected, we can see how clear this is compared to our colored mixture.

122
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:38,760
When the boiling points of the components in the mixture are very close, we can also

123
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:42,060
use another method called fractional distillation.

124
00:08:42,060 --> 00:08:47,040
This technique is similar to a simple distillation, except that we use a fractional column for

125
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:50,800
the vapors to travel through before they hit the condenser.

126
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:56,240
This column contains layers of glass beads that improve the separation between the liquids

127
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:58,080
that are being separated.

128
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:00,960
But for today, that's all we need to know about distillation.

129
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:03,920
Let's move forward and examine some other techniques.
